# Number of cusps of Γ₀(N) equals sum_{d∣ N}φ(gcd(d,N/d))

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.CuspSpace.card_cuspSpace_eq_cuspCount`
(`64ad9f87-b150-5ecf-b3a9-63fad04f9afe`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `b17bf4a0-4372-5461-84de-9c063fcbef3b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$. Consider the subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$ of
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, pushed forward along the map to $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q})$, so that
`Gamma0Q N` is its image as a subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q})$; this group acts on the
one-point compactification `OnePoint ℚ` of $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. on $\mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{Q})$
presented as $\mathbb{Q} \cup \{\infty\}$. The type [`ModularCurve.CuspSpace
N`](def/ModularCurve_CuspSpace.html#L103) is the quotient of $\mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{Q})$ by the orbit
relation for this action, that is the set of cusps $\Gamma_0(N) \backslash
\mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{Q})$. The assertion is that the cardinality of this quotient, taken as a
natural number via `Nat.card`, equals $$\mathrm{cuspCount}(N) = \sum_{d \mid N} \varphi\bigl(\gcd(d,
N/d)\bigr),$$ the sum being over the positive divisors $d$ of $N$ and $\varphi$ the Euler totient
function; in particular the quotient is finite, since `Nat.card` of an infinite type would be $0$
while the right-hand side is positive.

This is the classical count of the cusps of the modular curve $X_0(N)$, $\nu_\infty(N) = \sum_{d
\mid N} \varphi(\gcd(d, N/d))$. It feeds the numerical input for the genus computations and related
estimates for $X_0(N)$, being used for the count of orbits of the unipotent subgroup generated by
$T$ and for the bound on the rank of the space of parabolic homomorphisms in terms of the genus
formula.

